!batch
!echo
!limit=1000
!short=20
!constituents=1
!spell=0

% Focused tests for dictionary replacements of 4.0.knowledge rules.
% Keep examples small and diagnostic.  Artificial examples are fine when they
% exercise a replaced postprocessing rule without depending on unrelated
% grammar behavior.

% CONTAINS_ONE rule 6: I#a requires B#m or B#w.
% The broad to.r I*a+ fallback was removed, so these infinitival
% filler/gap constructions must still use licensed dictionary paths.

Tell me what to do.
Tell me which book to read.
Tell me which books to read.
Tell me how much water to drink.
The book to read is here.
What are you going to do?
What is there to do?
There is no nicer way than to leave now.
There is no nicer way to round off the evening than to have a quiet nightcap.

% CONTAINS_ONE rule 8: Qd direct question inversion is now constrained by the
% dictionary question paths and existential-there THR paths. Rule 9's PFc
% selector is unreachable in the current dictionary because PFc has no matching
% negative connector occurrence; no active sentence can exercise that stale
% PP check.

Does he drink?
Are you insane?
Is there a dog in the park?
Are there dogs in the park?

% CONTAINS_ONE rules 3 and 38: filler-it SFI inversion must have real
% question-root evidence.  Direct matrix questions keep SFIsi, while embedded
% how clauses use ordinary non-inverted order.

Is it important to turn off the computer?
Is it likely that Joe came?
I wonder how important it is to turn off the computer.
*I wonder how important is it to turn off the computer.

% CONTAINS_ONE rule 21: TSi that-clause complements require filler/expletive
% it evidence.  TSIC/PTSI carriers carry that evidence from subject, inverted
% subject, or object filler-it to the predicate that owns the TSi link.

It is important that women be ready when they make these choices.
Is it important that women be ready?
It may be important that women be ready.
It has been important that women be ready.
I want it to be important that women be ready.
I considered it important that women be ready.
It is imperative that assistance be provided through a variety of channels.
*Joe is important that women be ready when they make these choices.

% CONTAINS_ONE rule 23: TOi infinitive complements require filler/expletive
% it evidence.  TOIC/PTOI carriers carry that evidence to the predicate that
% owns the TOi link.  Tough-subject infinitives remain on TOt.

It is easy to use the program.
Is it easy to use the program?
It may be easy to use the program.
It has been easy to use the program.
I want it to be easy to use the program.
I considered it easy to use the program.
It is necessary to use the program.
It is easier to ignore the problem than it is to solve it.
Our program is easy to use.
*Our program is easier to use it than to understand.

% CONTAINS_ONE rule 22: QIi question-clause complements require
% filler/expletive it evidence.  QIIC/PQII carriers carry that evidence to
% the predicate that owns the QIi link; object-raising "it to be" uses
% TQII/IQII/PQII.

It is unknown whether he came.
It is unclear whether he came.
It is unknown why he came.
Is it unknown whether he came?
It may be unknown whether he came.
It has been unknown whether he came.
I made it clear how to use the program.
I want it to be obvious how to use the program.
*Joe is unknown how he came.
*The answer is unknown why he came.

% CONTAINS_ONE rule 24: Ci finite-clause complements require filler/expletive
% it evidence.  CIIC carries local direct it evidence to the predicate that
% owns the Ci link; copular, auxiliary, and object-raising paths use
% TCII/ICII/PPCII/PCII carriers.

It is likely he came.
Is it likely he came?
It is surprising he came.
It may be likely he came.
It has been likely he came.
I considered it likely he came.
I want it to be likely he came.
*Joe is likely he came.
*I considered Anne likely he came.
*Does it act likely that Joe came?
*Did it act likely that Joe came?

% CONTAINS_ONE rule 20: THi that-clause complements require filler/expletive
% it evidence.  THIC carries direct it evidence to the predicate that owns the
% THi link; object-raising and auxiliary paths use TTHI, ITHI, PPTHI, and
% PTHI carriers.  Clefts use a same-copula SFsi/SFIsi and THi branch.

It is likely that he came.
Is it likely that he came?
Does it seem likely that Joe came?
Can it be likely that Joe came?
Has it seemed likely that Joe came?
It happened that he came.
It seems that he came.
It was hoped that he came.
I made it clear that he came.
I want it to be likely that he came.
It was in Paris that Debussy first heard Balinese music.
Wasn't it in 1955 that Sally first met Joe?
*Joe is likely that he came.
*I made Anne clear that he came.
*Does it act likely that Joe came?
*Did it act likely that Joe came?
*Can it act likely that Joe came?

% CONTAINS_ONE rule 30: the old AFdi comparative branch on than.e is
% retired.  Valid comparative expletive-it examples parse through other
% certified complement or comparative paths; the lower ordinary subject
% example below must not be accepted through than.e--AFdi--is.

It is more likely that Joe died than that Fred died.
It is more likely that Joe died than it is that Fred died.
It is easier to ignore the problem than it is to solve it.
*It is more likely that Joe died than John is that Fred died.

% CONTAINS_NONE rule 72: filler-subject SF domains must not license ordinary
% infinitival and comparative continuations.  The former PP backstop is moved
% into local dictionary splits on to.r and than.e.

Failure to comply may result in dismissal.
It is easier to ignore the problem than it is to solve it.
It is more likely that Joe died than that Fred died.
*Absence to comply may result in dismissal.
*It is more likely that Joe died than John is that Fred died.

% CONTAINS_ONE rules 37, 38, and 39: broad expletive-it backstops are now
% redundant after the dedicated predicate certificates and the root-attached
% SFIsi question evidence split.

It is likely that he came.
I made it clear that he came.
*Joe is likely that he came.
*I made Anne clear that he came.

% CONTAINS_ONE rule 31: cleft-like O#i object paths require
% filler/expletive it evidence.  Direct copulas use SFsi/SFIsi with the Osi
% or Opi branch; auxiliary and object-raising paths carry this evidence with
% TOCL, IOCL, and PPOCL.  ROCL licenses the special case where the needed
% filler it appears inside the following clause.

It was John who stole the priceless documents.
It's John who stole the priceless documents.
Was it John who stole the priceless documents?
Isn't it John who stole the priceless documents?
It might be John who stole the priceless documents.
Could it be John who stole the priceless documents?
I want it to be John who stole the priceless documents.
It has been John who stole the priceless documents.
It may have been John who stole the priceless documents.
The shame of it is, is that it was a good idea.
*How likely is John that he will come?
*I believe Fred was John who stole the priceless documents.
*That is the man, in Joe's opinion, we should hire.

% CONTAINS_ONE rule 4: SXI also occurs in fronted locative/adverbial
% inversion licensed by PFb, not only in question or comparative paths.

Here am I.
Here was I.
There was I.

% CONTAINS_ONE rule 68: B#m extraction is licensed in dictionary paths.
% Wh/degree witnesses are carried by dedicated dictionary paths so ordinary
% determiner and quantity paths do not depend on the old "Bad use of
% gerund68" postprocessing rule.

Which dog did you chase?
How many do you want?
How many more times did you do it?
How many miles did you bike?
How much of a man is he?
How much effort did you give it?
What degree of trust do you put in him?
He won't divulge what type it is.
How big a department is it?
*The telling John to leave was stupid.
*How much more efficient programs are available.

% CONTAINS_NONE rule 71: Ws must not license extracted B#m or Ca paths.
% Subject and temporal-fragment how questions use dedicated dictionary
% certificate links, while extracted-object and non-inverted clause paths stay
% under the ordinary Wq/QI checks.

How many people died?
How much sugar is needed?
How long before you got home?
Approximately how long before you got home?
How much money did you earn?
How many times did you do it?
*How much money you earn
*How many times you did it
*How much of the book you read

% CONTAINS_ONE rule 5: Ws now appears only on subject-wh paths that are
% certified by the wh word or wh determiner expression itself.  Object-gap
% and non-subject paths use Wq or embedded-question connectors instead.

Who died?
Who bought your flat from?
Which dog chased you?
How many dogs ran?
How much oil spilled?
*Who to invite to the party.
*Which dog you chased.
*How quickly Joe ran.

% CONTAINS_NONE rule 73: filler-it inverted auxiliary paths must not license
% ordinary lower verbs.  Valid filler-it raising questions use IFI to reach
% the lower predicate; ordinary-subject raising keeps the existing I path.

Does it seem likely that Joe came?
Does it appear likely that Joe came?
Did it seem likely that Joe came?
Does Joe seem likely to come?
*Does it act likely that Joe came?
*Did it act likely that Joe came?

% CONTAINS_ONE rule 12: Wj requires Jw or JQ.
% Fronted prepositions in wh questions and relatives must be licensed by the
% matching wh object, including when the object is a multiword phrase.

With whom did you play tennis?
For whom were you mistaken?
To what do you owe your success?
Of which person were you speaking?
By what means will you arrive?

I know to what you owe your success.
Sophy wondered up to what number she should count.
Do you know in which room the procedure was performed?

The man with whom I play tennis is here.
The friend for whom Joe works is kind.
The subject about which Mary spoke was difficult.
The book of which I spoke was old.

With whom did you say Mary played tennis?
The man with whom John said Mary played tennis is here.
The man with whom Mary believes John played tennis is here.
With whom did Mary believe John played tennis?

That is the man for whom and with whom Joe works.

*With did you play tennis?
*To do you owe your success?
*Of person were you speaking?
*The man with whom I play tennis with is here.
*That is the man for whom and with Janet Joe works.

% CONTAINS_ONE rule 13: JQ requires Mj, Wj, MX#j, or MVp.
% Wh-preposition questions and relatives must keep a companion link in the
% same postprocessing domain.

By what means will you arrive?
Of which person were you speaking?
Sophy wondered up to what number she should count.
Do you know in which room the procedure was performed?
Exactly what mark did it reach?
Up to what mark did it reach?

% CONTAINS_ONE rule 14: Jw requires Mj, Wj, or MX#j.
% Ordinary J object paths must not match wh-object relations. The dictionary
% uses the distinct JW connector family for those wh-object continuations.

With whom did you play tennis?
For whom were you mistaken?
The friend for whom Joe works is kind.
That is the man for whom and with whom Joe works.

He replied with a yes.
He greeted me with a loud hallo!
He wanted to look at and listen to everything.

*That is the man for whom and with Janet Joe works.
*The man with whom I play tennis with is here.

% CONTAINS_ONE rules 10 and 11: Mj/MX#j require Jw or JQ.
% Prep-object relatives must keep a wh-object or question-preposition witness.

The friend for whom Joe works is kind.
The subject about which Mary spoke was difficult.
The subject, about which Mary spoke, was difficult.
That is the man for whom and with whom Joe works.
The book of which I spoke was old.

% CONTAINS_ONE rules 15 and 16: B#j and Jr must license each other.
% The relative "of whom" path uses OFJ so Jr cannot attach to an ordinary
% object and B#j cannot appear without the matching relative "of" relation.

The doctors, many of whom are surgeons, were angry.
The box contained many books, some of which were badly damaged.
The male of which bears a tail ran.

% CONTAINS_ONE rule 41: BIh no longer needs a separate PP check.
% The current BIh dictionary paths are already constrained enough for these
% embedded-clause examples; related predicate rules remain active.

It was as if he knew.
*It was as if knew.
I left because I was tired.
*I left because was tired.
I know when he left.

% CONTAINS_ONE rule 42: BIq predicate complements require a question-like
% subject or auxiliary-chain certificate.

The question is who we should invite.
The question may be who we should invite.
The issue has been whether we should leave.
This is who we should invite.
*The answer is who we should invite.
*The problem is who we should invite.
*The big mind on everybody's question is who killed OJ.

% CONTAINS_ONE rule 65: the MX#a postnominal-adjective rule was overbroad.
% Parenthetical adjective uses should not require the old complement-style
% criterion links.

The lady, unpleased, spoke sharply.
The lady, displeased, spoke sharply.
The dog, unsatisfied, barked loudly.

% CONTAINS_ONE rule 66: second-object connectors must not accept pronoun Ox-.
% Ditransitive-like frames keep noun/name/time/mass second objects but exclude
% pronouns from the O*n+ family.

I gave him Mary.
I gave him a rose.
I gave him for his birthday a very expensive present.
Please paint it all white.
John made himself familiar with the drawings.
You should hear him sing.
*I gave Mary him.
*I gave my brother it.
*I gave him for his birthday it.

% CONTAINS_ONE rule 62: Pa##j predicative adjectives require an object
% class licensed by the dictionary OAJ link.

I want it green.
I want John sober.
I want them ready.
I want these green.
The doctor declared him insane.
*I want a gift inexpensive.

% CONTAINS_ONE rule 63: postposed Ma adjectives need local complement
% licensing.
% CONTAINS_ONE rule 64: postposed Mam comparative adjectives need local
% complement licensing.

I need something useful.
The apartment available is small.
A man proud of his work arrived.
Voters angry about the economy will probably vote for Clinton.
Many Democrats unhappy about the economy but doubtful that Clinton can be elected probably won't vote at all.
We need a programmer knowledgeable about Lisp.
It is believed that even the troops loyal to Hussein will soon be forced to surrender.
He cried, his heart heavy with sadness.
*A gift inexpensive arrived.
*A child little arrived.
*A man proud arrived.
*Many Democrats unhappy but doubtful probably won't vote at all.
I saw a man taller than John.
The man taller than John arrived.
I need a box bigger than this.
A box bigger than this arrived.
A man taller and wider than Bill arrived.
*I saw a man taller.
*The man taller arrived.
*A man bigger quickly arrived.
*A man taller and wider arrived.

% CONTAINS_ONE rules 58 and 59: comparative-clause subjects need a
% compatible comparative antecedent.

She interviewed more programmers than were hired.
*She interviewed more programmers than was hired.
She spent more money than was budgeted.
She spent less money than was budgeted.
She spent more than was budgeted.
She spoke to more than was expected.
She interviewed as many programmers as were hired.
*She interviewed as many programmers as was hired.
More people came to the party than were expected.
More people came to the party than was expected.

% CONTAINS_ONE rule 56: comparative Cc clauses need a local comparative
% license. CONTAINS_NONE rule 78: comparative EAy must not pair with ordinary
% subordinate-as MVs. Temporal "as" now uses a verb-side MVSWH link instead,
% so comparative adjectives cannot accidentally license it.

I am as intelligent as John.
You are as sweet as sugar.
Any program as good as ours should be useful.
The coffee tastes the same as it did last year.
I slipped on the ice as I ran home.
*I am as intelligent as John does.

% CONTAINS_ONE rule 44: MVz comparative-as paths need local comparative
% evidence, but the old PP check was too broad for predicative/adjectival
% parenthetical "as" clauses.  The Pa branch now uses adjective-only MVZP.

Ridiculous as it seems, the tale is true.
He earns as much as was expected.
The claim, unclear as worded, deserves attention.
*I am as intelligent as John does.

% CONTAINS_ONE rule 47: MV#o comparative object-clause paths need local
% comparative object or measure evidence.  The MV#o branches now require CMPO.

The coffee tastes the same as it did last year.
I did as much as he did.
I earned as much as John earned.
*I am as intelligent as John does.

% CONTAINS_ONE rule 48: MV#c comparative auxiliary-clause paths need local
% comparative modifier, object, or measure evidence.  The MV#c branch now
% requires CMPCX or an existing comparative antecedent certificate.

I earn as much money as John does.
I earn as many dollars as John does.
I run as much as John does.
He runs as quickly as John does.
*I am as intelligent as John does.

% CONTAINS_ONE rule 43: the remaining MVt comparative check is redundant
% after the surrounding comparative certificate migrations.

I am more happy now than I was in college.
She spent more money than was budgeted.
*I earn as much money in a month than John earns in a year.

% CONTAINS_ONE rules 32s, 32p, 32u, 34, 35, and 36: existential "there"
% agreement is carried by dictionary connectors instead of PP checks; the old
% broad object-like OXt branch on there.r is removed.

There is a dog in the park.
There are dogs in the park.
There is water on the table.
Is there a dog in the park?
Are there dogs in the park?
There is going to be an important meeting in January.
There has been a problem.
There have been problems.
There will be a dog.
Will there be a dog?
Does there seem to be a dog in the park?
There seems to appear to have been likely to be a problem.
I want there to be a problem.
I want there to be more.
There goes the cutest guy ever.
Oh great, there went my ride.
*There is chasing dogs.
*There are a dog in the park.
*Are there a dog in the park?
*There has been problems.
*There seems to appear to have been likely to be problems.
*There seems to appear to have been likely to be stupid.

% CONTAINS_ONE rule 67: result-clause "that" needs a degree or such-noun
% certificate supplied by the modified word.

He ran so quickly that he fell.
It went almost so well that we thought we won!
I love her so much that I can't let her go.
The shuttle is so big that it has to be carried.
She presented her case with such eloquence that we could only admire her.
*He ran quickly that he fell.
*The shuttle is big that it fell.
*She presented her case with eloquence that we could only admire her.

% CONTAINS_ONE rules for controlled subjects: bare infinitive and present
% participle controlled-subject links use dictionary connector families
% instead of PP backstop checks.

You should hear John sing.
You should hear him sing.
I feel him breathing down my back.
John imagines himself singing from a mountaintop.
*You should hear him sings.
*I feel him breathes down my back.
*John imagines himself sings from a mountaintop.

% CONTAINS_NONE subject-inversion rule: directive-opener Qd questions require
% punctuation, so capitalized nouns cannot create the old Qd+MX path.

Joe, are you ready?
Anyhow, am I right?
It doesn't matter what Ted does.
*Joe doesn't matter what Ted does.
*Mary doesn't matter what Ted does.

% CONTAINS_ONE rule 7a: Wb bare-what openers require an inverted verb
% question certificate.

What did you think?
What were you thinking?
What the hell were you thinking?
*What the outcome is, I'm sure he'll still be popular.

% BOUNDED r-domain cleanup: possessive relative clauses should not need the
% old PP-level r-domain boundedness backstop.

The syndicates whose activities add to the cost left.
The syndicates whose activities add to the cost of business left.
The syndicates whose activities add to the cost of doing business left.
